1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,143
Ever notice hiccups always hit at the worst moment?

2
00:00:03,463 --> 00:00:13,701
They happen when your diaphragm suddenly spasms and your vocal cords snap shut right after - a few everyday habits set that off more than you'd think.

3
00:00:14,021 --> 00:00:15,807
Eating too fast is a big one.

4
00:00:16,127 --> 00:00:23,091
Wolfing down a meal makes you swallow air with it, and that trapped air stretches your stomach right against the diaphragm.

5
00:00:23,411 --> 00:00:28,804
A sudden temperature swing can do it too - hot soup followed by an ice-cold drink.

6
00:00:29,124 --> 00:00:32,465
The quick shift irritates the nerves near your diaphragm.

7
00:00:32,785 --> 00:00:35,200
Stress and excitement matter as well.

8
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:42,352
A jolt of nervousness sends extra signals through the vagus nerve, which connects straight down to the diaphragm.

9
00:00:42,672 --> 00:00:48,544
Just slow down and take smaller bites, and let hot and cold foods settle a few minutes apart.

10
00:00:48,864 --> 00:00:58,821
If hiccups start, hold your breath for 10 to 20 seconds and exhale slowly, or take a few slow sips of cold water.
